A||||9||mshill||Help towing an automatic...||||1||
Z||000000||mshill||10-11-2000||06:51 PM||||Has anyone used a tow dolly to transport an automatic fiero?  I understand the automatics should be towed backwards to keep the tranny fluid from overheating.  Can you put a car backwards on a tow dolly? <P>If not, I will just bite the bullet and get an auto carrier.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||10-11-2000||06:54 PM||||How far are you towing it? I guess you can put the car on a dolly backwards. Don't see any reason you couldn't. If you aren't going all that far though, I'd put it on a rollback.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||10-11-2000||07:52 PM||||About 120 miles.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||10-11-2000||07:57 PM||||The general rule of thumb that I use when towing cars is 35 mph for 15 miles.  I drive tow truck and this is a good rule of thumb.  Also today even some of the stick trannys can't be towed at high speed of for long periods.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||10-11-2000||07:58 PM||||The general rule of thumb that I use when towing cars is 35 mph for 15 miles.  I drive tow truck and this is a good rule of thumb.  Also today even some of the stick trannys can't be towed at high speed of for long periods.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-11-2000||08:19 PM||||WT III and I pulled the Grape Ape ('85GT/auto) back from Chicago last winter.  About 160 miles or so if memory serves.  We rented a dolly from U-Haul and towed it backwards.  We removed the sunroof (didn't want the wind to catch it) but other than that, didn't do anything special that I can remember.  I don't remember going slow.  Standard highway speeds all the way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||10-11-2000||08:38 PM||||I personally would pull auto or maunal Firoes backwards on a tow dolly.  I towed a 85 SE 5 sp with the rear wheels up from Louisiana to Huntsville AL going normal highway speed the whole way.  Ed Parks has probably towed 100's of cars with the rear end on the dolly.  I just used the steering wheel lock plate to keep the front wheels straight, but I would probably recommend tying the wheel down as a safety measure.<P>With the dolly I rented, you needed to check to be sure the undercarriage doesn't hit the rear edge of the tow dolly platform.  This is caused by the trailer hitch on the tow vehicle being too low for the dolly.  This tends to put the back of the wheel trays high and sometimes into the underframe or collant tubes.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-11-2000||09:16 PM||||Never trust the steering column to hold the wheels straight for towing. That's just bad. It's not made for that. The steering wheel must always be tied off. <P>In Fiero that's a pain. One thing you can do is loosen the seats front hold down nuts so you can get a rope under then snug them so they can't pop off.<P>If you are going to tow any distance make sure the dolly comes with a set of lights to stick on the car. You don't want some jackass to run into it first time you tap the brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||10-11-2000||09:42 PM||||I have towed several Fieros (and a Saturn) with my GT.  I always put the butt of the Fiero on the dolly.  You want as much weight as possible up front.  <P>Whether or not it's a good idea (depending on who you talk to).....I don't see a problem trusting the steering column lock.  I have towed a couple of Fieros cross-country (2000+ miles) with no problems.  The steering column lock did its job and no problems were incurred.  Since you are only going 120 miles you shouldn't have anything to worry about.  Just plan your stops and start slowing down before you need to!     ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mshill||10-12-2000||01:15 AM||||Guys... Thanks for all the responses.  I will be towing with my GMC Safari Van (4.3L Vortec) and I towed a full auto transport plus Fiero, but the dolly weighs about on third of the transport and thought it would be a lot easier on the van.  So backwards on a dolly it will be.  It looks like I am getting my third Fiero on Sunday.||||reg||1
